  \item \textbf{Regression Estimation} Suppose we simulate regression data with 
  Exponential error. Note that we can still fit an OLS line -- that is the line 
  described here minimizes the sum of squared errors -- but the inference about 
  the coefficients is compromised.
<<size="scriptsize", warning=F, message=F>>=
# Specify Coefficients
beta0 <- -4.2
beta1 <- 1.3
# Specify Error Distribution
mu.epsilon <- 0
sigma.epsilon<-sqrt(5)
# Simulate Regression Data
set.seed(7272)
n <- 50
x <- runif(n=n, min=-5, max=5)
epsilon <- rexp(n=n, rate = 1/sigma.epsilon)
y <- beta0 + beta1*x + epsilon
dat <- tibble(x=x, y=y)
# Fit the Linear Model
mod <- lm(y ~ x, data=dat)
summary(mod)
@
\begin{enumerate}
  \item We can fit an OLS regression model without the normality of error terms.
  Write a function for computing 
  \[Q(\beta_0,\beta_1) =  \sum_{i=1}^{n} \left[y_i - \left(\beta_0 + \beta_1 x_i\right)\right]^2.\]
  Use the \texttt{optim()} function to minimize this function with respect
  to $\beta_0$ and $\beta_1$.
  
  <<warning = F, message= F, size = 'scriptsize'>>=


Q <- function(betas){
  
  beta0 <- betas[1]
  
  beta1 <- betas[2]
  
  residuals <- (y - (beta0 + beta1*x))^2
  
  sum <- sum(residuals)
  
}

  (min_betas <- optim(c(1,1), Q)$par)


@
  
  \item Compute the OLS estimates using matrix multiplication.
  \[\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}} = \left(\mathbf{x}'\mathbf{x}\right)^{-1} \mathbf{x}'\mathbf{y}\]
  
  <<warning=F, message=F, size='scriptsize'>>=

x_1 <- tibble(x0 = rep(1, 50), 
              x1 = x) |>
  as.matrix()

xxinv <- solve(t(x_1)%*%x_1)

xty <- t(x_1)%*%y

(betas <- xxinv %*% xty)

@
  
  \item Note that the OLS coefficient estimates are off -- specifically the 
  intercept -- due to the transgression of conditions for use, specifically the 
  error terms are Exponentially distributed. Write a likelihood for fitting a 
  regression model with Exponential error and use \texttt{optim()} to find the 
  MLE estimates for $\beta_0$, $\beta_1$, and $\lambda$.\\
  \textbf{Hint:} Note that residuals can only be positive under this setting, so 
  you must select initial values of $\beta_0$ and $\beta_1$ that ensure all 
  observations are \emph{above} the regression line.
  
  <<warning=F, message=F, size='scriptsize'>>=

reg.loglike <- function(par, y, x, p=2, neg=F){
  betas <- matrix(par[-length(par)]) # (betas, sigma)
  lambda <- par[length(par)]
  # Compute residual
  resid <- y - (x %*% betas)
  # Compute log likelihood
  loglik <- sum(log(dexp(resid, rate = lambda)))
  # return +/- loglikelihood
  ifelse(neg, -loglik, loglik)
}

# Setup Data
y <- matrix(y)
x <- cbind(matrix(data=1, nrow=nrow(dat), ncol=1),
           matrix(x))
# Optimize
(par <- optim(par = c(-10,0,1),
      fn = reg.loglike,
      neg=T,
      y=y, x=x)$par)




@
  
  \item Create a \texttt{ggplot} that plots the OLS and the MLE regression lines
  on top of a scatterplot of the data. 
  
\begin{figure}[H] 

\centering
  
  <<message=F, warning=F, size='scriptsize', fig.dim = c(6,4)>>=


OLS <- function(x){
  betas[1,1] + x*betas[2,1]
}

MLE <- function(x){
  par[1] + x*par[2]
}


ggplot(data = dat, aes(x=x, y=y)) + 
  geom_point() + 
  geom_function(fun = OLS, aes(color = "OLS")) + 
  geom_function(fun = MLE, aes(color = "MLE")) + 
  theme_bw() + 
  labs(title = "MLE vs. OLS Models", 
       colour = "")

@
\label{model.comparison}
\caption{Comparison of MLE vs. OLS Model Estimates}
\end{figure}
  
  
\end{enumerate}
