  \item \textbf{(Real Application)} \cite{Akinola18} investigate how group 
  diversity impacts performance. Specifically, they hypothesized that higher 
  testosterone, which correlates positively with competitiveness and negatively 
  with cooperation, aids homogeneous groups, while lower testosterone benefits 
  diverse ones.
  
  The researchers collected data on $n=370$ MBA students from Columbia 
  Business School, who were randomly assigned to 74 groups of 3-6 people.
  Each group completed a week-long task to advise a hypothetical blood-testing 
  lab, and their relative performance on the task was measured by a combination 
  of the profitability and number of contracts held by their lab at the end of 
  the 7-day period.

  You can load the cleaned data as follows.
<<size="scriptsize", warning=F, message=F>>=
dat <- read_csv(file = "cleaned_team_data.csv")
@
  \begin{enumerate}
      \item Create a scatterplot matrix for the performance, time of day, team
      size, proportion of females, log testosterone, and homogeneity.\\
      \textbf{Note:} The homogeneity is recorded as \texttt{fau} in the data file.
      
      \begin{figure}[H]
      \centering
      <<warning=F, message=F, size='scriptsize', fig.dim = c(8,8)>>=
library(GGally)
upper <- list(continuous = "points", discrete = wrap("colbar", size = 0), combo = "box_no_facet", na = "na")
lower <- list(continuous = "cor", discrete = wrap("colbar", size = 0), combo = "box_no_facet", na = "na")

ggpairs(data = dat |> 
          dplyr::select(final_performance, time_of_day, team_size, prop_female, log_testosterone, fau) |>
          mutate(team_size = as.factor(team_size), 
                 time_of_day = as.factor(time_of_day)), 
        upper = upper, lower = lower) + 
  theme_bw() + 
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 60, vjust = 1, hjust = 1)) + 
  scale_fill_grey() + 
  scale_color_grey()

@

\label{ggpairs}
\caption{Scatterplot Matrix of the Data}
\end{figure}
      
      \item Fit the following regression model.
          \begin{align*}
          \textrm{Performance} &= \textrm{Time of Day} + \textrm{Team size} + \textrm{Prop. female}\\
                       &~~~~~~~~~~ + \log(\textrm{Testosterone}) + \textrm{Homogeneity}\\
                       &~~~~~~~~~~ + \log(\textrm{Testosterone}) \times \textrm{Homogeneity}
           \end{align*}
           
<<warning=F, message=F, size='scriptsize'>>=


mod <- lm(final_performance ~ time_of_day + team_size + prop_female + log_testosterone + fau + log_testosterone*fau, data = dat)


@
           
           
      \item Conduct a residual analysis for the regression model. Do you have
      any concerns?
      
      \begin{figure}[H]
      \centering
      
      <<warning=F, message=F, size='scriptsize', fig.dim=c(6,5)>>=

library(effectsize)
source(file = "plotResiduals.R")
source(file = "plotInfluence.R")

plotResiduals(mod)


@

\label{residuals}
\caption{Residual Analysis of the Model}

\end{figure}
      
      \textcolor{red}{The residuals appear approximately normal with the histogram and density roughly following the gaussian assumption. The qqplot fits well except for the tails. The residuals are scattered across observation numbers and are also scattered across fitted values with a tendency to be more clustered towards fitted values of 0. There is one point that might have an unusually large residual compared to the rest of the data. }
      
      
      \item Conduct a leverage/influence analysis for the regression model. Do
      you have any concerns?
      
      \begin{figure}[H]
      \centering
      
      <<warning=F, message=F, size='scriptsize', fig.dim=c(6,4)>>=

plotInfluence(mod)

@

\label{influence}
\caption{Leverage/Influence Analysis of the Model}

\end{figure}

\textcolor{red}{There are 2 points who appear to have large leverage and influence on the model based on the Leverage and DFFITs plots. There are also 2-3 points that have quite large negative residuals.}
      
      \item Does the model support the hypothesis that ``diversity will boost 
      performance among groups collectively low in testosterone but harm 
      performance among groups collectively high in testosterone"?\\
      \textbf{Note:} We will learn more complicated approaches to answer this
      question later in the semester. For now, use the techniques we discussed
      during the first lecture on regression.
      
      <<warning=F, message=F, size='scriptsize'>>=
library(xtable)

sum_table <- data.frame(summary(mod)$coefficients) |>
  mutate(significance = case_when(Pr...t.. < 0.001 ~ "***", 
                                  Pr...t.. >= 0.001 & Pr...t.. < 0.01 ~ "**", 
                                  Pr...t.. >= 0.01 & Pr...t.. < 0.05 ~ "*", 
                                  Pr...t.. >= 0.05 ~ ""))

effect_size <- omega_squared(mod, partial = T)
sum_table$`Effect Size` <- c("", round(effect_size$Omega2_partial,3))

sum_table$`Interpretation` <- c("",interpret_omega_squared(effect_size$Omega2_partial, rules = "cohen1992"))

colnames(sum_table) <- c("Estimate", "Standard Error", "t", "p-value", "Significance", "Effect Size", "Interpretation")
rownames(sum_table) <- c("Intercept", "Time of Day", "Team Size", "Proportion Female", "log(Testosterone)", "Homogeneity", "log(Testosterone) : Homogeneity")

sum_table$`p-value` <- round(sum_table$`p-value`, 3)
sum_table$`p-value` <- ifelse(sum_table$`p-value` == 0, "<0.001", sum_table$`p-value`)

sum_table <- xtable(sum_table,label = "sum.tab", caption = "Model Summary", digits = 3)


@

<<results="asis", echo=F>>=
print(sum_table,
table.placement = "H", include.rownames=T, size = "scriptsize")
@

<<warning=F, message=F, size='scriptsize'>>=

#Low testosterone
coef.low <- data.frame(t(coef(mod))) |>   # A data frame of coefficients
    mutate(X.Intercept. = X.Intercept. + (-sd(dat$log_testosterone)) * log_testosterone, # Adjust intercept and slope
           fau = fau + (-sd(dat$log_testosterone)) * log_testosterone.fau) |>
    dplyr::select(-c(log_testosterone,log_testosterone.fau)) # remove testosterone coefficients

# When testerone is average
coef.avg <- data.frame(t(coef(mod))) |>  # A data frame of coefficients
    mutate(X.Intercept. = X.Intercept. + (mean(dat$log_testosterone)) * log_testosterone, # Adjust intercept and slope
           fau = fau  + (mean(dat$log_testosterone)) * log_testosterone.fau)|>
  dplyr::select(-c(log_testosterone,log_testosterone.fau)) # remove testosterone coefficients

# High testosterone
coef.high <- data.frame(t(coef(mod))) |> # A data frame of coefficients
    mutate(X.Intercept. = X.Intercept. + (sd(dat$log_testosterone)) * log_testosterone, # Adjust intercept and slope 
           fau = fau + (sd(dat$log_testosterone)) * log_testosterone.fau)|>
  dplyr::select(-c(log_testosterone,log_testosterone.fau)) # remove testosterone coefficients


interaction_tab <- tibble(`Testosterone Level` = c("low (-1 SD)", "average (mean)", "high (+1 SD)"), 
                          `Homogeneity Estimate` = c(coef.low$fau, coef.avg$fau, coef.high$fau))


interaction_tab <- xtable(interaction_tab,label = "interaction.tab", caption = "Interaction Summary", digits = 3)


@

<<results="asis", echo=F>>=
print(interaction_tab,
table.placement = "H", include.rownames=F, size = "normalsize")
@

\textcolor{red}{According to the linear model shown in Table: \ref{sum.tab}, the only factor with a statistically discernible effect on team performance is the interaction between the log(testosterone) and the homogeneity of the group ($t = 4.188$, $p < 0.001$, $\omega_{p} = 0.183$). We see in Table: \ref{interaction.tab} that the estimate of homogeneity on team performance changes substantially depending on testosterone level. The estimate of homogeneity on team performance is $-0.333$ when team testosterone levels are low (-1SD) and $0.440$ when team testosterone levels are high (+1SD). From this we can estimate that increased diversity hurts teams that have higher testosterone, while helping teams that have lower testosterone.}
      
      
  \end{enumerate}
  
\end{enumerate}
